# Registry Adapter product

This directory owns the Adapter product contract, the execution plan that
decomposes it, and the product-local verification scripts. Read the
workspace `AGENTS.md`, this file, and the two documents below before
changing the product or any `registry-adapter*` crate.

- `CONCEPT.md` is the product contract: the artifact model, the engine
  (a bounded Rust port of Apache Camel, with its port table in section
  8.10), writes and failure semantics, authorization,
  audit, configuration formats, and the quality verification every gate
  implements. On any conflict, `CONCEPT.md` wins over every other
  document in this directory.
- `DELIVERY-PLAN.md` is the execution plan: the wave model, stream
  ownership, the eleven rules every agent works under, the review protocol, and each increment's
  definition of done and acceptance commands. It never widens the
  contract.

## Area ownership

| Area | Owns |
|---|---|
| `crates/registry-adapter-core` | Source-neutral Adapter model, wire DTOs, `adapter.*` problem codes, and pure authoring checks; no I/O (it depends on `registry-platform-httputil` only for its pure header predicates and bounds) |
| `crates/registry-adapter` | Adapter runtime: the governed facade, the Camel-port engine (exchange, routes, EIP processors, components), the security surface, the audit journal, and the idempotency store |
| `crates/registry-adapterctl` | Adapter authoring and operator tooling and the `adapterctl` binary |
| `crates/registry-adapter-connectors` | The parked BReg connector over `registry-breg-client`, behind an off-by-default cargo feature; linked by no facade binary and no job path while the governed-ingestion delivery design is decided. The dependency gate names exactly what it may reach |
| `products/adapter/` | `CONCEPT.md`, `DELIVERY-PLAN.md`, schemas, profiles, packs, examples, fixtures, and product scripts |

`CONCEPT.md` section 8.1 fixes the platform reuse: the runtime reaches
`registry-platform-script`, `-oidc`, `-httputil`, `-ratelimit`,
`-config`, `-yaml`, `-authcommon`, `-crypto`, and `-canonical-json`, and never another
product's runtime, tooling, or protocol types. Product logic (routes, EIP
processors, mapping, disclosure) never moves into a platform crate. The
generic primitives under `throttle`, `circuitBreaker`, and redelivery live in
`registry-platform-ratelimit` and `registry-platform-resilience` (D13); the
processors and the redelivery loop stay here. This program changes no
Evidence crate; its other platform edits are format registration in
`products/platform/` and additive constructors accepted in review.
`[ingestion]` The one exception to "never another product's protocol
types" is the connector crate above: it reaches the maintained product
clients only, never a product runtime, CLI, or database crate, and
`products/adapter/scripts/check_dependency_direction.py` enforces that
boundary mechanically (`CONCEPT.md` section 17.5). No facade binary and
neither engine crate links the connector crate or any product client.

## Gates

Run from the worktree root; every cargo invocation carries
`CARGO_TARGET_DIR=<target-dir> PATH="$HOME/.cargo/bin:$PATH"`, your own
target directory for `<target-dir>`.

```bash
products/adapter/scripts/check-contracts.sh
products/adapter/scripts/check_dependency_direction.py
products/adapter/scripts/test-postgres.sh
```

`check-contracts.sh` runs the verification floor from `DELIVERY-PLAN.md`:
`cargo fmt --check` over the workspace plus clippy and tests over the
adapter crates (`-p registry-adapter -p registry-adapter-core
-p registry-adapterctl`), the generated schemas held to their generator,
and the platform configuration-format lint. `test-postgres.sh` runs the
adapter crates' `postgres-test` suites: without
`ADAPTER_TEST_DATABASE_URL` it starts and removes its own loopback
PostgreSQL 17 container; with the variable set it uses that server as
given. The PostgreSQL suites themselves land with the journal in
Increment 1 wave 1. Schema drift, `check-port-table.py`, and fixture
checks join at the integration waves, as `DELIVERY-PLAN.md` assigns
them.

## Verify commands

```bash
CARGO_TARGET_DIR=<target-dir> PATH="$HOME/.cargo/bin:$PATH" cargo fmt --check
CARGO_TARGET_DIR=<target-dir> PATH="$HOME/.cargo/bin:$PATH" cargo clippy -p registry-adapter -p registry-adapter-core -p registry-adapterctl --all-targets -- -D warnings
CARGO_TARGET_DIR=<target-dir> PATH="$HOME/.cargo/bin:$PATH" cargo test -p registry-adapter -p registry-adapter-core -p registry-adapterctl --locked
```

Every adapter diagnostic is value-free under an `adapter.*` code
(CONCEPT.md section 11, CFG-SEC-3): a report names the artifact, the
JSON Pointer, and the code, never a value that could carry secret or
environment material. Configuration formats follow
`products/platform/CONFIG-CONVENTIONS.md`; the `Adapter` envelope prefix
and the five v1alpha1 format kinds are registered through the platform
change Increment 0 wave 1 (stream A2) makes in
`products/platform/config-formats.yaml`.
# Registry Stack configuration conventions

These rules apply to every configuration format Registry Stack defines: the
files adopters author, the runtime files operators deploy, and the files our
tools generate for a person or another tool to read. A person who has learned
one product's files should be able to predict how every other product's files
look, how they are read, and how a mistake is reported.

The rules are normative. **MUST** rules are enforced by the gate the
[Enforcement summary](#enforcement-summary) gives them. A mechanical gate fails
CI when a change breaks its rule; where the gate is review, the reviewer cites
the rule ID. **SHOULD** rules are reviewed by people; a deviation needs a
reason in the pull request. A rule whose reason is not evident states it, so
a reviewer can tell a real exception from a habit.

Product guides decide what a format means. These conventions decide how it is
written and read. When a product guide and this page disagree on spelling,
parsing, or diagnostics, this page wins; fix the guide.

## Scope

In scope:

- **Authored files** describe what a registry, service, or package is:
  `registry.yaml`, `module.yaml`, `casework.yaml`, `scheduling.yaml`,
  `messaging.yaml`, Render bundles, Discovery origins and mappings, Evidence
  bundles and authoring files, fixtures, journeys, and the files beside them.
  They are reviewed in version control, are deterministic, and never hold an
  environment value or a secret. A value that differs per environment (an
  issuer, an endpoint) is named in the authored file by a local identifier and
  bound in an operator file.
- **Operator files** describe where and how a deployment runs: every
  `runtime.yaml`, development client files, and operator request files.
- **Generated files** are of two kinds. A **read-back format** is one a
  Registry Stack tool reads again (module locks, package manifests, receipts,
  checkpoints, development state); it follows every rule except CFG-SCHEMA-2
  and CFG-SCHEMA-6, which cover the files people edit. An **output format**
  is written for people or for tools we do not own and is never read back
  (explain output, `--format json` reports); it is registered with
  `reader: none`, follows CFG-ENV, CFG-NAME, CFG-ID, CFG-QTY, and CFG-EMPTY,
  and is exempt from CFG-YAML-1, CFG-CHECK-1, CFG-SCHEMA-2, and CFG-SCHEMA-6.
  A **build artifact** is a read-back format that its own product's build
  writes and reads again and that no one edits (the compiled package model in
  BReg's `package.json`). It is registered with `audience: generated` and
  `buildArtifact: true`; the registry entry is held to the code like any
  other, and no rule of this convention applies to the format.
- Authored and operator **JSON** documents (scenarios, migration descriptors,
  backup bindings) follow every rule that is not YAML-specific, including the
  envelope. A JSON document that is a request or response body of a product
  API (`examples/inputs/*.json`, operator request files) is a payload: the API
  contract governs it, not these conventions. Authored JSON is parsed strictly
  (CFG-YAML-2) and never required to be canonical bytes; a tool that digests it
  canonicalizes it first.

Out of scope, recorded as exception classes in
[Exceptions](#exceptions):

- formats another project owns (OpenAPI, JSON Schema, LinkML, ThunderID
  resources, `SHA256SUMS`, GitHub workflows, release tooling);
- exchange data models whose member names a published specification fixes
  (Registry Manifest metadata documents) and record data (JSONL imports);
- HTTP problem codes, which belong to each product's API contract.

Also out of scope:

- repository tooling metadata read only by repository scripts
  (`config-formats.yaml`, the exceptions register, release manifests), which
  those scripts' tests govern.

## Concepts

| Term | Meaning |
|---|---|
| Format | One document shape with one `apiVersion` and `kind`, owned by one product. |
| Envelope | The `apiVersion` and `kind` pair every document carries. |
| Local identifier | A name an author chooses in our files for something our files define (an entity, a profile, a template, a destination). |
| External identifier | A name defined elsewhere that our files quote (an OIDC client id, a claim name, a scope, a URN). |
| Reference | A member whose value names an item defined elsewhere in the same file or another file. |
| Sentinel | A reserved string written in place of a list or value to state an open choice explicitly, such as `unrestricted`. |
| Diagnostic | One reported problem: severity, code, location, message, and the fix. |
| Stability | `promised`: covered by API stability; `experimental`: shipped, may change in a minor release with a `BREAKING:` note; `unpromised`: no promise (fixtures, journeys, development clients, ctl data files, authoring files). |
| Format registry | `products/platform/config-formats.yaml`, the machine-readable list of every format, its schema, its reader, and its check command. |
| Exceptions register | `products/platform/config-conventions-exceptions.yaml`, every recorded deviation with its class and resolution. |

## 1. Files and envelopes

**CFG-ENV-1 (MUST). Every document carries `apiVersion` and `kind`.** Both
are plain strings at the top level, in any position, and the reader checks
them before it decodes any other member. Neither may come from `${...}`
substitution. A document missing either is refused at its root node, with
the empty path (`config.missing-envelope`); a document of another format, or
at a version the reader does not read, is refused with a message that names
the expected `apiVersion` and `kind`. When both are wrong, `kind` is reported
(`config.wrong-kind`). The `apiVersion` is reported beside it only when it is
a version the reader lists as retired (`config.retired-api-version`), so the
author learns of both in one run; any other wrong `apiVersion` is not
reported until `kind` is right.
*Why:* a reader can refuse the wrong file with a precise message ("this is a
`CaseworkFixture`, `casework check` reads a `CaseworkProject`"), editors can
pick a schema, and a format can be versioned on its own.
*Enforced by:* the shared reader's envelope check; the format registry lint
(each schema file describes one `apiVersion` and declares both members as
`const`; a format read at several versions has one schema per version,
CFG-SCHEMA-3).

**CFG-ENV-2 (MUST). `apiVersion` is
`id.registrystack.org/formats/<product>/<format>/<version>`.** `<product>` is
the product token (CFG-ENV-3). `kind` matches
`^(BReg|Casework|Coordinator|Documents|Scheduling|Messaging|Discovery|Render|Evidence|Manifest|Adapter|Platform)([A-Z][a-z0-9]+)+$`,
with acronyms written as words (`Mcp`, `Oid4vci`). `<format>` derives from
`kind`: remove the product prefix; remove a trailing `Config`; split the rest
before each uppercase letter; lowercase each word and join with `-`. The
result is non-empty and unique within the product. `<version>` matches
`^v[1-9][0-9]*((alpha|beta)[1-9][0-9]*)?$`.

| `kind` | `apiVersion` (before its version) |
|---|---|
| `CaseworkProject` | `id.registrystack.org/formats/casework/project/` |
| `BRegProject` | `id.registrystack.org/formats/breg/project/` |
| `BRegRuntimeConfig` | `id.registrystack.org/formats/breg/runtime/` |
| `BRegDataImportCheckpoint` | `id.registrystack.org/formats/breg/data-import-checkpoint/` |
| `CaseworkFixture` | `id.registrystack.org/formats/casework/fixture/` |
| `AdapterProfile` | `id.registrystack.org/formats/adapter/profile/` |
| `AdapterRuntimeConfig` | `id.registrystack.org/formats/adapter/runtime/` |

The `apiVersion` is a name, not a URL: readers compare it literally and never
fetch it. The identifier catalog may document it at the same path under
`https://`.
*Why:* `id.registrystack.org` is the one namespace for every identifier the
stack mints, and its catalog already promises that an identifier is never
reused for another meaning and stays documented after it is retired, which is
what a format version needs. The product and format segments are the ones the
schema `$id` uses (CFG-SCHEMA-3), so one derivation names both.

**CFG-ENV-3 (MUST). `kind` is PascalCase, starts with the owning product's
prefix, and is unique across the stack.**

| Prefix | Product token |
|---|---|
| `BReg` | `breg` |
| `Casework` | `casework` |
| `Coordinator` | `coordinator` |
| `Documents` | `documents` |
| `Scheduling` | `scheduling` |
| `Messaging` | `messaging` |
| `Discovery` | `discovery` |
| `Render` | `render` |
| `Evidence` | `evidence` |
| `Manifest` | `manifest` |
| `Adapter` | `adapter` |
| `Platform` | `platform` |

Supporting services take their parent product's prefix and add their own name
to the format (`BRegMcpRuntimeConfig` is `breg/mcp-runtime`,
`EvidenceOid4vciRuntimeConfig` is `evidence/oid4vci-runtime`).
*Why:* a kind names the file without its path; `Fixture` alone would be
ambiguous in a repository that holds three products.
*Enforced by:* the format registry lint (pattern and uniqueness).

**CFG-ENV-4 (MUST). One document per file, one format per document.** A
leading `---` and a final `...` are accepted. A second YAML document (`---`
after content) is refused. A file that is empty or holds only comments is
refused as `config.missing-envelope`, naming the expected members.
*Enforced by:* the shared reader (`yaml.multiple-documents`).

**CFG-ENV-5 (SHOULD). Tools write `.yaml` files named after the format**
(`registry.yaml`, `runtime.yaml`, `module.yaml`), and write `apiVersion` and
`kind` as the first two keys. Readers accept whatever path they are given; the
envelope, not the extension, identifies the format.

**CFG-ENV-6 (MUST). A product's top-level authored file, the one its project
check starts from (CFG-CHECK-2), is `<Prefix>Project`,** format `project`
(`BRegProject`, `CaseworkProject`, `SchedulingProject`, `MessagingProject`).
It names the project in a top-level `project` block holding the shared
`ProjectIdentity` members, `id` (a local identifier) and `version` (text); the
block may add project-wide members of the product's own. A product whose
top-level authored file is a bundle of files rather than a project (Render,
Evidence) names it `<Prefix>Bundle`, format `bundle`.
*Why:* a person who has written one product's project file knows how the next
one opens, and "package" stays the name of the built artifact.
*Enforced by:* the format registry lint (a `project` format's kind ends in
`Project`, and its `project` block carries the `ProjectIdentity` members).

## 2. The YAML subset

Registry Stack reads YAML 1.2 with one parser, the shared reader in
`registry-platform-yaml`, and accepts a strict subset. Every refusal below
reports the file, line, and column.

**CFG-YAML-1 (MUST). One reader.** Every format in the registry is read
through `registry-platform-yaml` (runtime files through
`RuntimeConfigLoader`, which builds on it). No crate deserializes YAML with
`serde_norway` or any other YAML crate directly; serialization (writing YAML)
is unaffected. JSON documents are read by the same reader, as YAML 1.2 flow
content, so their refusals carry the same codes. The language server may parse
incomplete buffers with its own parser for editor features, but reports
diagnostics from the shared reader.
*Why:* the audit that produced these rules found five parse pipelines whose
behavior depended on the target type: duplicate keys refused in one product
and silently last-wins in another, `id: null` read as the text `"null"`, line
// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0

//! The pass, the only unit of work of the governed-ingestion job
//! (`products/adapter/CONCEPT.md` section 17.3 and the governed-ingestion
//! concept section 3): one bounded page read from the saved position,
//! the per-item comparison against the item table, the hand-offs
//! through the destination seam, and the saved position. The seven
//! steps, in order:
//!
//! 1. Take the job's lease; a held lease refuses the pass with the
//!    position untouched.
//! 2. Read one page at the saved position (at the first page when this
//!    pass is a rescan); a page fault stops with the position unchanged.
//! 3. For each item, project and digest it, stage its row, and hand it
//!    off: a new item or a changed digest delivers, a still-pending item
//!    re-delivers identical bytes, and a settled or refused item skips
//!    unless this pass is a rescan retrying it.
//! 4. An acknowledged hand-off settles the row with the destination's
//!    reference.
//! 5. An item-scoped refusal is recorded with its code and the pass
//!    continues, until too many consecutive refusals with one code stop
//!    the pass with the position unchanged.
//! 6. A deployment-level answer stops the pass with the position
//!    unchanged.
//! 7. A fully settled page saves the page's next cursor, or clears the
//!    position at a finite end, and calls the destination's settle
//!    contract.

use std::collections::BTreeMap;
use std::time::{Duration, SystemTime};

use deadpool_postgres::Pool;
use registry_adapter::engine::job::{run_job_page, JobPageFailure, JobProgram};
use serde_json::Value;

use crate::destination::{DeliverableItem, HandOffOutcome, JobDestination, SettledPage};
use crate::projection;
use crate::store::{self, StagedClassification};
use crate::trigger::JobState;

/// How many consecutive item-scoped refusals with one code stop the
/// pass, when the operator names no limit. The governed-ingestion
/// concept's stop rule: a schema or policy change can refuse every item
/// alike, which would otherwise run the position to the end of the
/// source.
pub const DEFAULT_REFUSAL_LIMIT: u32 = 20;

/// The options one pass runs under.
#[derive(Clone, Debug)]
pub struct PassOptions {
    /// The consecutive item-scoped refusals with one code that stop the
    /// pass with the position unchanged.
    pub refusal_limit: u32,
    /// Run this pass from the first page regardless of the saved
    /// position: the rescan that finds edits an ordered position cannot
    /// see, and the only pass that retries refused items. A completed
    /// rescan stamps its own time, which gates the next one through the
    /// source's declared delay.
    pub rescan: bool,
    /// Read and deliver but write nothing: the operator preview. No
    /// lease is taken, no row is staged, no position is saved, and the
    /// report says what a real pass would have done.
    pub dry_run: bool,
}

impl Default for PassOptions {
    fn default() -> Self {
        Self {
            refusal_limit: DEFAULT_REFUSAL_LIMIT,
            rescan: false,
            dry_run: false,
        }
    }
}

/// Why a pass stopped before its page settled. Every reason is
/// value-free: a closed code or a structural fact.
#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub enum StopReason {
    /// Another pass holds the job's lease; nothing ran.
    LeaseHeld,
    /// The page read failed under its closed code: the page fault's
    /// `adapter.job.page-*` code, the route failure's class, or the
    /// store's own refusal.
    PageFault {
        /// The closed code of the failure.
        code: String,
    },
    /// The destination answered for the deployment, not the item.
    DestinationStop {
        /// The destination's own code.
        code: String,
    },
    /// The destination refused this many items in a row with one code.
    RefusalLimit {
        /// The code the consecutive refusals shared.
        code: String,
    },
}

impl StopReason {
    /// The value-free stop text the job state row records. A
    /// destination's code is recorded as itself, so status names what
    /// the destination said and nothing more.
    #[must_use]
    pub fn stop_text(&self) -> String {
        match self {
            StopReason::LeaseHeld => "lease-held".to_string(),
            StopReason::PageFault { code } => format!("page-fault:{code}"),
            StopReason::DestinationStop { code } => format!("destination-stop:{code}"),
            StopReason::RefusalLimit { code } => format!("refusal-limit:{code}"),
        }
    }
}

/// What one pass did, value-free: counts, refusal codes, and why it
/// stopped, never an item value.
#[derive(Clone, Debug, Default)]
pub struct PassReport {
    /// The items the page carried.
    pub items_read: u32,
    /// Items staged as new: no row existed.
    pub items_new: u32,
    /// Items staged as changed: the digest moved.
    pub items_changed: u32,
    /// Items re-delivered under the same digest: a still-pending row, or
    /// a refused row this rescan retried.
    pub items_redelivered: u32,
    /// Items skipped: settled, or refused outside a rescan.
    pub items_skipped: u32,
    /// Hand-offs the destination acknowledged.
    pub items_acknowledged: u32,
    /// Hand-offs the destination refused, item-scoped.
    pub items_refused: u32,
    /// The item-scoped refusals by the destination's own code.
    pub refusals_by_code: BTreeMap<String, u32>,
    /// Malformed input lines the page's decoders skipped, value-free.
    pub malformed_lines: u32,
    /// Why the pass stopped before the page settled, when it did.
    pub stopped: Option<StopReason>,
    /// Whether the job's interval schedule had already slipped when the
    /// pass ended: true means the due time had passed, most tellingly on
    /// a lease the pass could not take.
    pub overdue: bool,
}

/// The state the pass reads once, under the lease or the dry-run's
/// read-only glance.
struct PassState {
    position: Option<Value>,
}

/// Run one bounded pass of the job against the item store and the
/// destination. See the module documentation for the seven steps.
///
/// The lease's time to live covers the page deadline the compiled
/// profile enforces, plus the margin an operator needs to see a stalled
/// pass in status before the lease frees; a pass that crashes leaves
/// the lease to expire, and the next pass starts no earlier.
pub async fn run_pass(
    db: &Pool,
    program: &JobProgram,
    destination: &dyn JobDestination,
    options: PassOptions,
) -> PassReport {
    let job = &program.job;
    let job_id = job.job.id.as_str();
    let mut report = PassReport::default();
    let mut client = match db.get().await {
        Ok(client) => client,
        Err(error) => return store_refused(report, &error.to_string()),
    };
    // The dry run writes nothing: it reads the state to report what a
    // real pass would do from it, and skips the lease.
    let state = if options.dry_run {
        read_pass_state(&mut client, job_id, program, &mut report).await
    } else {
        take_pass_state(&mut client, job_id, program, &mut report).await
    };
    let Some(state) = state else {
        return report;
    };
    // Step 2: read one page, at the saved position, or at the first page
    // when this pass is a rescan.
    let cursor = if options.rescan {
        None
    } else {
        state.position.clone()
    };
    let page = match run_job_page(program, cursor).await {
        Ok(result) => {
            report.malformed_lines = result.malformed_lines;
            result.page
        }
        Err(failure) => {
            return finish_stopped(db, job_id, &options, report, page_failure_reason(&failure))
                .await;
        }
    };
    report.items_read = page.items.len() as u32;
    // Steps 3 to 6: per item, compare, stage, deliver, record.
    let mut consecutive = ConsecutiveRefusals::new(options.refusal_limit);
    for item in &page.items {
        let digest = projection::item_digest(&job.projection, &item.value);
        let staged = if options.dry_run {
            store::peek_item(&client, job_id, &item.key, &digest, options.rescan).await
        } else {
            store::stage_item(
                &mut client,
                job_id,
                &item.key,
                &digest,
                options.rescan,
                SystemTime::now(),
            )
            .await
        };
        let staged = match staged {
            Ok(staged) => staged,
            Err(error) => {

exec
/bin/zsh -lc "sed -n '220,620p' crates/registry-adapter-job/src/pass.rs; sed -n '1,420p' crates/registry-adapter-job/src/store.rs" in /Volumes/CodexWorktrees/adapter-components
 succeeded in 0ms:
            Err(error) => {
                return finish_stopped(db, job_id, &options, report, store_refused_reason(&error))
                    .await;
            }
        };
        let Some(staged) = staged else {
            report.items_skipped += 1;
            continue;
        };
        match staged.classification {
            StagedClassification::New => report.items_new += 1,
            StagedClassification::Changed => report.items_changed += 1,
            StagedClassification::Redelivered => report.items_redelivered += 1,
        }
        let deliverable = DeliverableItem {
            key: staged.key.clone(),
            sequence: staged.sequence,
            digest: staged.digest.clone(),
            prior_run_id: staged.prior_run_id,
            admitted_before: staged.admitted_before,
            projected: projection::project(&job.projection, &item.value),
        };
        match destination.deliver(&job.job, &deliverable).await {
            HandOffOutcome::Acknowledged { run_id } => {
                report.items_acknowledged += 1;
                consecutive.reset();
                if !options.dry_run {
                    if let Err(error) = store::record_acknowledged(
                        &client,
                        job_id,
                        &staged.key,
                        run_id.as_deref(),
                        SystemTime::now(),
                    )
                    .await
                    {
                        return finish_stopped(
                            db,
                            job_id,
                            &options,
                            report,
                            store_refused_reason(&error),
                        )
                        .await;
                    }
                }
            }
            HandOffOutcome::Refused { code } => {
                report.items_refused += 1;
                *report.refusals_by_code.entry(code.clone()).or_insert(0) += 1;
                if let Some(limit_code) = consecutive.refused(code.clone()) {
                    return finish_stopped(
                        db,
                        job_id,
                        &options,
                        report,
                        StopReason::RefusalLimit { code: limit_code },
                    )
                    .await;
                }
                if !options.dry_run {
                    if let Err(error) = store::record_refused(
                        &client,
                        job_id,
                        &staged.key,
                        &code,
                        SystemTime::now(),
                    )
                    .await
                    {
                        return finish_stopped(
                            db,
                            job_id,
                            &options,
                            report,
                            store_refused_reason(&error),
                        )
                        .await;
                    }
                }
            }
            HandOffOutcome::StopPass { code } => {
                return finish_stopped(
                    db,
                    job_id,
                    &options,
                    report,
                    StopReason::DestinationStop { code },
                )
                .await;
            }
        }
    }
    // Step 7: the page settled. Save the position, stamp the schedule,
    // release the lease, and call the destination's settle contract. A
    // cursor the route carried out as JSON null is no cursor: the
    // position clears, the finite end.
    let next = page.next.as_ref().filter(|value| !value.is_null());
    if !options.dry_run {
        if let Err(error) = store::complete_pass(
            &client,
            job_id,
            next,
            report.items_read == 0,
            options.rescan,
            SystemTime::now(),
        )
        .await
        {
            return finish_stopped(db, job_id, &options, report, store_refused_reason(&error))
                .await;
        }
    }
    destination
        .settle(
            &job.job,
            &SettledPage {
                next: next.cloned(),
                items_read: report.items_read,
            },
        )
        .await;
    report
}

/// The stopped-pass ending: record the stop with the position untouched
/// and return the report. The dry run records nothing, because it
/// writes nothing.
async fn finish_stopped(
    db: &Pool,
    job_id: &str,
    options: &PassOptions,
    mut report: PassReport,
    reason: StopReason,
) -> PassReport {
    if !options.dry_run {
        if let Ok(client) = db.get().await {
            let _ = store::stop_pass(&client, job_id, &reason.stop_text(), SystemTime::now()).await;
        }
    }
    report.stopped = Some(reason);
    report
}

/// The stop reason for a store refusal: the store's own account of the
/// failure, carried under one closed code, value-free.
fn store_refused_reason(error: &store::StoreError) -> StopReason {
    StopReason::PageFault {
        code: format!("adapter.job.store-refused:{error}"),
    }
}

/// A pass that could not reach its own state row: the report carries
/// the failure and nothing ran.
fn store_refused(mut report: PassReport, reason: &str) -> PassReport {
    report.stopped = Some(StopReason::PageFault {
        code: format!("adapter.job.store-refused:{reason}"),
    });
    report
}

/// Read the state the pass runs from, without taking the lease, and
/// stamp the report's overdue flag from it. `None` means the report
/// already carries its ending.
async fn read_pass_state(
    client: &mut deadpool_postgres::Object,
    job_id: &str,
    program: &JobProgram,
    report: &mut PassReport,
) -> Option<PassState> {
    let row = match store::read_state(client, job_id).await {
        Ok(row) => row,
        Err(error) => {
            *report = store_refused(std::mem::take(report), &error.to_string());
            return None;
        }
    };
    let row = row.unwrap_or_default();
    report.overdue = overdue(&row, program);
    Some(PassState {
        position: row.position,
    })
}

/// Take the lease and read the state under it: the pass's step 1.
/// `None` means the report already carries its ending, a held lease or
/// a refused store.
async fn take_pass_state(
    client: &mut deadpool_postgres::Object,
    job_id: &str,
    program: &JobProgram,
    report: &mut PassReport,
) -> Option<PassState> {
    if let Err(error) = store::ensure_state(client, job_id).await {
        *report = store_refused(std::mem::take(report), &error.to_string());
        return None;
    }
    let row = match store::read_state(client, job_id).await {
        Ok(row) => row,
        Err(error) => {
            *report = store_refused(std::mem::take(report), &error.to_string());
            return None;
        }
    };
    let row = row.unwrap_or_default();
    // The overdue flag reads the state as this pass found it: on a held
    // lease, where no pass ran at all, it is the number that matters.
    report.overdue = overdue(&row, program);
    match store::take_lease(client, job_id, lease_ttl(program)).await {
        Ok(true) => {}
        Ok(false) => {
            report.stopped = Some(StopReason::LeaseHeld);
            return None;
        }
        Err(error) => {
            *report = store_refused(std::mem::take(report), &error.to_string());
            return None;
        }
    }
    // The state row's projection digest follows the job's current one, so
    // status can show a projection edit; the items re-flag through their
    // own digests, which a new projection changes.
    if row.projection_digest.as_deref() != Some(program.job.projection_digest().as_str()) {
        if let Err(error) =
            store::sync_projection_digest(client, job_id, &program.job.projection_digest()).await
        {
            *report = store_refused(std::mem::take(report), &error.to_string());
            return None;
        }
    }
    Some(PassState {
        position: row.position,
    })
}

/// The lease's time to live: the profile's page deadline plus the
/// margin an operator needs to see a stalled pass before the lease
/// frees.
fn lease_ttl(program: &JobProgram) -> Duration {
    Duration::from_millis(program.profile().page_deadline_milliseconds) + Duration::from_secs(60)
}

/// The closed reason of a failed page read: the page fault's own code,
/// or the route failure's class.
fn page_failure_reason(failure: &JobPageFailure) -> StopReason {
    if let Some(code) = failure.code {
        return StopReason::PageFault {
            code: code.to_string(),
        };
    }
    StopReason::PageFault {
        code: failure
            .class
            .map(|class| class.class().to_owned())
            .unwrap_or_else(|| "adapter.job.page-failure".to_string()),
    }
}

/// The consecutive-same-code refusal counter of one pass. Refusals with
/// the same code stack toward the limit; a different code restarts the
/// count at one, because the stop rule targets one refusal cause running
/// through every item alike; and any acknowledgement clears it.
struct ConsecutiveRefusals {
    limit: u32,
    code: Option<String>,
    count: u32,
}

impl ConsecutiveRefusals {
    fn new(limit: u32) -> Self {
        Self {
            limit,
            code: None,
            count: 0,
        }
    }

    fn reset(&mut self) {
        self.code = None;
        self.count = 0;
    }

    /// Count one refusal with its code; `Some` when the count reached
    /// the limit, carrying the code the pass stops on.
    fn refused(&mut self, code: String) -> Option<String> {
        if self.code.as_deref() == Some(code.as_str()) {
            self.count += 1;
        } else {
            self.code = Some(code.clone());
            self.count = 1;
        }
        if self.count >= self.limit {
            self.code.clone()
        } else {
            None
        }
    }
}

/// Whether the job's interval schedule had already slipped when the pass
/// started: the trigger's due time, read against the state as found, has
/// passed. A settled or stopped pass stamps the clock it ended on, so
/// this answers true mostly where the pass could not run at all, the
/// held lease above.
fn overdue(row: &store::JobStateRow, program: &JobProgram) -> bool {
    let view = JobState {
        position: None,
        projection_digest: None,
        lease_expires: None,
        last_pass_at: row.last_pass_at,
        last_pass_stop: None,
        last_rescan_at: row.last_rescan_at,
        idle_streak: u32::try_from(row.idle_streak.max(0)).unwrap_or(u32::MAX),
        error_streak: u32::try_from(row.error_streak.max(0)).unwrap_or(u32::MAX),
    };
    crate::trigger::next_due(&program.job, &view, SystemTime::now())
        .is_some_and(|due| due == Duration::ZERO)
}

#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::*;

    #[test]
    fn the_stop_texts_name_the_reason_and_nothing_from_the_items() {
        assert_eq!(StopReason::LeaseHeld.stop_text(), "lease-held");
        assert_eq!(
            StopReason::PageFault {
                code: "adapter.job.page-key".to_string()
            }
            .stop_text(),
            "page-fault:adapter.job.page-key"
        );
        assert_eq!(
            StopReason::DestinationStop {
                code: "auth.rejected".to_string()
            }
            .stop_text(),
            "destination-stop:auth.rejected"
        );
        assert_eq!(
            StopReason::RefusalLimit {
                code: "schema.required".to_string()
            }
            .stop_text(),
            "refusal-limit:schema.required"
        );
    }

    #[test]
    fn the_refusal_limit_stops_on_one_code_run_and_a_new_code_restarts_it() {
        let mut consecutive = ConsecutiveRefusals::new(3);
        assert_eq!(consecutive.refused("a".to_string()), None);
        assert_eq!(consecutive.refused("a".to_string()), None);
        // A different code restarts the count: the stop rule is one code
        // running through the items.
        assert_eq!(consecutive.refused("b".to_string()), None);
        assert_eq!(consecutive.refused("b".to_string()), None);
        assert_eq!(consecutive.refused("b".to_string()), Some("b".to_string()));
    }

    #[test]
    fn an_acknowledgement_clears_the_refusal_run() {
        let mut consecutive = ConsecutiveRefusals::new(2);
        assert_eq!(consecutive.refused("a".to_string()), None);
        consecutive.reset();
        assert_eq!(consecutive.refused("a".to_string()), None);
        assert_eq!(consecutive.refused("a".to_string()), Some("a".to_string()));
    }

    #[test]
    fn a_limit_of_one_stops_at_the_first_refusal() {
        let mut consecutive = ConsecutiveRefusals::new(1);
        assert_eq!(consecutive.refused("a".to_string()), Some("a".to_string()));
    }
}
// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0

//! The item table and the job state row
//! (`products/adapter/CONCEPT.md` section 17.2): the PostgreSQL store of
//! the governed-ingestion job host. One row per item carries identity
//! and progress and no payload; one row per job carries the opaque
//! source position, the lease, and the schedule counters. Every write is
//! stamped from the runtime clock or the database clock, never a
//! default, and every failure names its cause without repeating a
//! connection string or a value.

use std::collections::BTreeMap;
use std::fmt;
use std::str::FromStr as _;
use std::time::{Duration, SystemTime};

use deadpool_postgres::{
    GenericClient, Manager, ManagerConfig, Object, Pool, RecyclingMethod, Runtime,
};
use registry_platform_config::SecretResolver;
use serde_json::Value;
use tokio_postgres::Error as PgError;

/// Every schema version, in the order it is applied. Each later
/// migration takes the next number.
pub(crate) const MIGRATIONS: [(i64, &str); 1] =
    [(100, include_str!("store/migrations/0100_job_items.sql"))];

/// The newest schema version this release applies.
pub(crate) const SUPPORTED_SCHEMA_VERSION: i64 = MIGRATIONS[MIGRATIONS.len() - 1].0;

/// Shared advisory-lock key for schema migration. Its ASCII bytes spell
/// "ajobstat", distinct from every other migration lock in the product.
pub(crate) const MIGRATION_LOCK_KEY: i64 = 0x616a_6f62_7374_6174;

/// Why a store write, read, or migration failed. Neither the pool nor
/// the driver repeats the connection string in its message, so naming
/// the cause costs no credential; no message repeats an item value.
#[derive(Debug)]
pub enum StoreError {
    /// The database holds a job schema newer than this release applies.
    SchemaNewer {
        /// The newest version the database applied.
        found: i64,
        /// The newest version this release supports.
        supported: i64,
    },
    /// The migration lock was not held when it was released.
    MigrationLock,
    /// A pool or connection could not be built or reached.
    Pool(String),
    /// The driver's own account of what went wrong.
    Query(PgError),
}

impl fmt::Display for StoreError {
    fn fmt(&self, formatter: &mut fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> fmt::Result {
        match self {
            StoreError::SchemaNewer { found, supported } => write!(
                formatter,
                "the adapter job database holds schema version {found}, newer than the version {supported} this release applies; this release cannot serve it"
            ),
            StoreError::MigrationLock => write!(
                formatter,
                "the adapter job migration lock was not held when it was released"
            ),
            StoreError::Pool(reason) => write!(
                formatter,
                "the adapter job database pool failed: {reason}"
            ),
            StoreError::Query(error) => {
                write!(formatter, "the adapter job store query failed: {error}")
            }
        }
    }
}

impl std::error::Error for StoreError {
    fn source(&self) -> Option<&(dyn std::error::Error + 'static)> {
        match self {
            StoreError::SchemaNewer { .. } | StoreError::MigrationLock | StoreError::Pool(_) => {
                None
            }
            StoreError::Query(error) => Some(error),
        }
    }
}

impl From<PgError> for StoreError {
    fn from(error: PgError) -> Self {
        StoreError::Query(error)
    }
}

/// Apply every pending job-store migration under the shared advisory
/// lock, refusing a database this release cannot serve. Running it
/// again, or twice at once, applies nothing twice. The migration table
/// is this crate's own, so the job host's schema versions move beside,
/// never against, the facade journal's.
///
/// # Errors
///
/// A newer schema, a failed migration, or a lost lock refuses.
pub async fn migrate(client: &impl GenericClient) -> Result<(), StoreError> {
    client
        .query_one("SELECT pg_advisory_lock($1)", &[&MIGRATION_LOCK_KEY])
        .await?;
    let applied = apply_migrations(client).await;
    let released = client
        .query_one("SELECT pg_advisory_unlock($1)", &[&MIGRATION_LOCK_KEY])
        .await;
    applied?;
    if released?.get::<_, bool>(0) {
        Ok(())
    } else {
        Err(StoreError::MigrationLock)
    }
}

async fn apply_migrations(client: &impl GenericClient) -> Result<(), StoreError> {
    client
        .batch_execute(
            "CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS adapter_job_schema_migrations (\
             version bigint PRIMARY KEY CHECK (version > 0),\
             applied_at timestamptz NOT NULL)",
        )
        .await?;
    let applied = client
        .query("SELECT version FROM adapter_job_schema_migrations", &[])
        .await?
        .into_iter()
        .map(|row| row.get::<_, i64>(0))
        .collect::<Vec<_>>();
    if let Some(found) = applied.iter().copied().max() {
        if found > SUPPORTED_SCHEMA_VERSION {
            return Err(StoreError::SchemaNewer {
                found,
                supported: SUPPORTED_SCHEMA_VERSION,
            });
        }
    }
    for (version, migration) in MIGRATIONS {
        if applied.contains(&version) {
            continue;
        }
        client.batch_execute(migration).await?;
        client
            .execute(
                "INSERT INTO adapter_job_schema_migrations(version,applied_at) VALUES($1,now())",
                &[&version],
            )
            .await?;
    }
    Ok(())
}

/// The job store block of the operator runtime config (`store:`): the
/// item table's single connection reference. The job store runs its
/// migrations on the same connection it serves from, so the block
/// carries no separate migration member; the facade's database block
/// (journal and idempotency pools) is a facade member the job document
/// refuses. Secret references only, resolved by the host, never a URL
/// in the document.
#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq, serde::Deserialize)]
#[serde(rename_all = "camelCase", deny_unknown_fields)]
pub struct JobStoreConfig {
    /// The runtime connection reference of the item table's database.
    pub runtime_url_ref: registry_platform_yaml::SecretReference,
    /// The root certificate the connection trusts beside the platform
    /// roots; absent, the platform roots alone.
    #[serde(default)]
    pub trusted_root_certificate_ref: Option<registry_platform_yaml::SecretReference>,
    /// Plaintext connections for the loopback test database only;
    /// refused in any build without this crate's `postgres-test`
    /// feature, the way the facade database refuses it.
    #[serde(default)]
    pub test_only_plaintext: bool,
}

/// Build the connection pool the way the facade's serving runtime does:
/// the URL resolved from the operator runtime config's secret reference,
/// TLS required, and plaintext allowed only where the facade allows it,
/// in a build with this crate's `postgres-test` feature and the
/// operator's `testOnlyPlaintext` member. The same pool carries the
/// store's migrations and its passes.
///
/// # Errors
///
/// An unresolvable reference, an invalid URL, or an unbuildable pool
/// refuses, naming the block and never the URL.
pub fn build_pool(config: &JobStoreConfig, resolver: &SecretResolver) -> Result<Pool, StoreError> {
    let invalid = |what: &str| StoreError::Pool(format!("store: {what}"));
    let reference = &config.runtime_url_ref;
    let protected = resolver
        .resolve(reference.as_str())
        .map_err(|_| invalid("a connection reference could not be resolved"))?;
    let url = std::str::from_utf8(protected.expose_secret())
        .map_err(|_| invalid("the connection url is not text"))?;
    let mut postgres = tokio_postgres::Config::from_str(url)
        .map_err(|_| invalid("the connection url is invalid"))?;
    if postgres.get_user().is_none() || postgres.get_dbname().is_none() {
        return Err(invalid("the connection url names no user or database"));
    }
    let manager_config = ManagerConfig {
        recycling_method: RecyclingMethod::Verified,
    };
    #[cfg(feature = "postgres-test")]
    let manager = if config.test_only_plaintext {
        postgres.ssl_mode(tokio_postgres::config::SslMode::Disable);
        Manager::from_config(postgres, tokio_postgres::NoTls, manager_config)
    } else {
        postgres.ssl_mode(tokio_postgres::config::SslMode::Require);
        Manager::from_config(postgres, tls_connector(config, resolver)?, manager_config)
    };
    #[cfg(not(feature = "postgres-test"))]
    let manager = {
        if config.test_only_plaintext {
            return Err(invalid("plaintext connections are refused in this build"));
        }
        postgres.ssl_mode(tokio_postgres::config::SslMode::Require);
        Manager::from_config(postgres, tls_connector(config, resolver)?, manager_config)
    };
    Pool::builder(manager)
        .max_size(4)
        .wait_timeout(Some(Duration::from_secs(5)))
        .create_timeout(Some(Duration::from_secs(5)))
        .recycle_timeout(Some(Duration::from_secs(5)))
        .runtime(Runtime::Tokio1)
        .build()
        .map_err(|error| invalid(&format!("the pool could not be built: {error}")))
}

/// The TLS connector for the job host's database connections: the
/// configured root certificate when the store block names one, the
/// platform's own roots otherwise.
fn tls_connector(
    config: &JobStoreConfig,
    resolver: &SecretResolver,
) -> Result<tokio_postgres_rustls::MakeRustlsConnect, StoreError> {
    let invalid = |what: &str| StoreError::Pool(format!("store: {what}"));
    let _ = rustls::crypto::ring::default_provider().install_default();
    let Some(reference) = &config.trusted_root_certificate_ref else {
        let (connector, warnings) =
            tokio_postgres_rustls::MakeRustlsConnect::with_native_certs()
                .map_err(|_| invalid("no platform root certificate could be loaded"))?;
        for warning in warnings {
            tracing::warn!("a platform root certificate was skipped: {warning}");
        }
        return Ok(connector);
    };
    let protected = resolver
        .resolve(reference.as_str())
        .map_err(|_| invalid("a certificate reference could not be resolved"))?;
    let mut roots = rustls::RootCertStore::empty();
    use rustls::pki_types::pem::PemObject as _;
    for certificate in rustls::pki_types::CertificateDer::pem_slice_iter(protected.expose_secret())
    {
        roots
            .add(certificate.map_err(|_| invalid("a trusted certificate is unreadable"))?)
            .map_err(|_| invalid("a trusted certificate is unusable"))?;
    }
    let client = rustls::ClientConfig::builder()
        .with_root_certificates(roots)
        .with_no_client_auth();
    Ok(tokio_postgres_rustls::MakeRustlsConnect::new(client))
}

/// The last hand-off's outcome, the closed status set of the item table.
#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, Eq, PartialEq)]
pub enum ItemStatus {
    /// Handed off, the answer not yet recorded.
    Pending,
    /// The destination accepted the item.
    Acknowledged,
    /// The destination refused the item with its own code.
    Refused,
}

impl ItemStatus {
    /// The wire spelling of the status, the operator output's.
    #[must_use]
    pub fn as_str(self) -> &'static str {
        match self {
            ItemStatus::Pending => "pending",
            ItemStatus::Acknowledged => "acknowledged",
            ItemStatus::Refused => "refused",
        }
    }

    fn from_str(status: &str) -> Option<Self> {
        match status {
            "pending" => Some(ItemStatus::Pending),
            "acknowledged" => Some(ItemStatus::Acknowledged),
            "refused" => Some(ItemStatus::Refused),
            _ => None,
        }
    }
}

/// One stored item row, the fields the pass and the operator surface
/// read. There is no payload member: the table holds none.
#[derive(Clone, Debug)]
pub struct ItemRecord {
    /// The source item's key.
    pub key: String,
    /// The plain digest of the last hand-off's bytes.
    pub last_digest: String,
    /// The change count, from 1.
    pub sequence: i64,
    /// The last hand-off's outcome.
    pub status: ItemStatus,
    /// The destination's item-scoped refusal code, for a refused row.
    pub code: Option<String>,
    /// The destination's reference for this digest's hand-off.
    pub run_id: Option<String>,
    /// The destination's reference for a previous, different-digest
    /// hand-off of this key.
    pub prior_run_id: Option<String>,
    /// Whether an earlier digest's receipt may have been lost.
    pub admitted_before: bool,
    /// When the row first appeared.
    pub first_seen: SystemTime,
    /// When the row was last written.
    pub last_seen: SystemTime,
}

/// What the pass stages before a delivery: the decision, made against
/// the stored row, that this hand-off is new, changed, or a re-delivery.
#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, Eq, PartialEq)]
pub(crate) enum Decision {
    /// No row existed: sequence 1.
    DeliverNew,
    /// The digest changed: the sequence advances, the prior hand-off's
    /// reference moves beside the row.
    DeliverChanged,
    /// The same digest hands off again: a still-pending row, or a
    /// refused row a rescan retries. The sequence does not move.
    DeliverRedelivery,
    /// The item is unchanged and settled or refused outside a rescan.
    Skip,
}

/// The stored state a decision reads.
#[derive(Clone, Debug)]
pub(crate) struct PriorRow {
    /// The stored digest.
    pub last_digest: String,
    /// The stored change count.
    pub sequence: i64,
    /// The stored status.
    pub status: ItemStatus,
    /// The stored reference.
    pub run_id: Option<String>,
    /// Whether an earlier digest's receipt may have been lost.
    pub admitted_before: bool,
}

/// The pass's decision for one item against its stored row: a new item
/// or a changed digest delivers; the same digest on a pending row
/// re-delivers identical bytes; the same digest on an acknowledged row
/// skips, and on a refused row skips unless this pass is a rescan, the
/// only pass that retries refused items.
pub(crate) fn decide(prior: Option<&PriorRow>, digest: &str, retry_refused: bool) -> Decision {
    match prior {
        None => Decision::DeliverNew,
        Some(prior) if prior.last_digest == digest => match prior.status {
            ItemStatus::Pending => Decision::DeliverRedelivery,
            ItemStatus::Acknowledged => Decision::Skip,
            ItemStatus::Refused if retry_refused => Decision::DeliverRedelivery,
            ItemStatus::Refused => Decision::Skip,
        },
        Some(_) => Decision::DeliverChanged,
    }
}

/// The store's answer to staging one item: what the pass delivers and
/// how the report classifies it.
#[derive(Clone, Debug)]
pub struct StagedItem {
    /// The item's key.
    pub key: String,
    /// The change count after staging: 1 for a new item, the prior count
    /// plus one for a changed digest, the prior count for a re-delivery.
    pub sequence: i64,
    /// The plain digest this hand-off carries.
    pub digest: String,
    /// The prior hand-off's reference, moved beside the row on a change.
    pub prior_run_id: Option<String>,
    /// Whether a prior digest's receipt may have been lost.
    pub admitted_before: bool,
    /// How the staged delivery classifies in the report.
    pub classification: StagedClassification,
}

/// How one staged delivery classifies in the pass report.
#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, Eq, PartialEq)]
pub enum StagedClassification {
    /// The item had no row.
    New,
    /// The digest changed.
    Changed,
    /// The same digest re-delivered.
    Redelivered,
}

/// Stage one item for delivery: make the decision and, when it
/// delivers, write the row `pending` in one transaction before the
/// caller touches the destination. A crash after this call and before
/// the destination's answer leaves a pending row whose next pass
/// re-delivers identical bytes; that is the pass's at-least-once
/// hand-off, and the destination's dedup duty follows from the retry
/// identity of the seam.
///
/// # Errors
///
/// A failed read, write, or commit refuses; the caller treats the item
/// as undelivered.
pub async fn stage_item(
    client: &mut Object,
    job_id: &str,

