## 17. Governed ingestion `[ingestion]`

Applied 2026-10-31 as a contract amendment under the governed-ingestion
component brief. The delivery runner, the ledger, and the mapping
implementation are a separate brief; this section carries the contracts
they call: the engine boundary, the job profile, the item contract, the
read step, the reviewed packs, and the native product connectors. Nothing
in this section is claimed until its acceptance row in section 17.7 passes
against a real deployment; until then every component it names is
feature-gated, off by default, and served by no facade binary.

### 17.1 Layers and source independence

An ingestion job tracks source items through acquisition, mapping,
delivery, and reconciliation. The layers stay separate: a reviewed pack
route acquires; a declared bounded stage groups; the ledger admits work
items before source progress advances; a pure mapping produces candidate
operation inputs; target resolution reads explicitly and never guesses
among ambiguous matches; a fixed delivery state machine owns each remote
attempt; and a native connector turns one prepared attempt into exactly
one typed client call. One place per rule: protocol normalization lives in
the pack, field meaning lives in the mapping, and matching policy lives in
the job definition.

Source protocol, transport, and data format are separate. CSV is a format,
SFTP is a transport, PostgreSQL is a database connector, and KoboToolbox
is an application API. A CSV file received by SFTP uses the same decoder
and the same mapping as an identical local CSV file. Every source takes
one shape: a job route, called with a cursor, that returns the item
contract of section 17.2. `http` and `postgres` fit as sends; `file` and
`sftp` fit through the job-only read step. `check` refuses all three job
components in every facade route, so the facade gains no file or SQL path.

### 17.2 Engine boundary, job profile, item contract, read step

The route interpreter already runs without a facade profile through
`run_route` and its siblings. A job compiler separates the deployment
assembly: it loads a pack, an `AdapterJob` definition, and a runtime
config, and exposes the job's source route as a runnable program with no
listener, no database requirement, no OIDC issuer, and no inbound Record
shape. The interpreter is not rewritten, no fake request is invented, and
the exchange starts with a null body and the job cursor under a closed
property name, bounded by the job profile's page deadline.

The job profile is explicit and bounded, separate from the facade
ceilings of section 8.8, which are unchanged. Under the platform caps of
`registry-platform-httputil` (D39): a job send's per-attempt timeout stays
at or below the platform-frozen 10 seconds, and a job endpoint's
`maximumResponseBytes` may be raised up to the platform's 16 MiB
response-body cap. The profile also bounds what the facade never sees: the
page deadline of one route run, the read size and page size of the read
step, and the row and field limits of the decoders. Every read has a
count, byte, and deadline bound, tested at the edge.

The item contract is a core type: `{items: [{key, revision?, value}],
next}`. `key` identifies the source item inside the job's configured
namespace; `revision` is the observed source version when reliable, plus
or as a digest of captured content; `value` is the captured item; `next`
is the resumption cursor, absent at the end of a finite scan. A source
route declares its contract: whether the scan is `finite` or `ongoing`,
and the recovery semantics of `next`, from a closed set (`exact-replay`,
where a replayed cursor re-reads the same bytes, such as a file digest and
position, and `key-deduplicated`, where upstream pages may shift between
reads and the consumer must deduplicate by key). `check` enforces the
declaration; the job entry validates every produced page against the
contract shape and bounds before it is returned.

The read step is a bounded port of Camel's `pollEnrich`: a job-only step
over a static URI (`file:<endpointId>`, `sftp:<endpointId>`) with a
mandatory timeout, one bounded page per read. This keeps every source one
route shape: a job route whose steps poll or send and whose final body is
the item page. It is refused by `check` in every facade route. The
alternative form, a job-route `from: file:`, is recorded as rejected: it
would give the engine a second consumer model beside `rest` for no gain,
and the port table row records the choice.

### 17.3 Native product connectors

Connectors live outside `registry-adapter`, in one feature-gated crate
that no facade binary links; only the companion executable and tests
depend on it. A connector calls the maintained product client only: never
hand-authored HTTP, never a product database, never a product runtime or
tooling crate. Each registered operation names, through a descriptor, its
input and output schemas, body kind (JSON or binary reference), product
binding, credential requirements, mutation and precondition behavior,
idempotency requirements, and recovery and completion rules. Only
implemented, enabled operations are advertised; an unsupported operation
fails during checking. The connector validates and constructs typed client
requests, supplies and persists command keys (client libraries never
invent them), and retains the client's prepared forms where they exist.

The operation contract, as a signature shape:

```text
describe() -> descriptor
prepare(input, binding, key) -> prepared   // exact, persistable, no bearer token
send(prepared) -> outcome                  // accepted | pending | completed | refused | uncertain
recover(prepared) -> outcome               // this operation's rule; never a new key
observe(correlation) -> native lifecycle state, where the family has one
```

Outcomes keep the product's native detail beneath the shared reporting
categories. Each family carries its own recovery rule from the client's
contract: BReg Start and Submit recover through `BRegPreparedAction` and
`BRegPreparedProposal`; the receipt-horizon refusals (`409
idempotency.key-reused`, `410 idempotency.expired`) surface as outcomes,
never as silent key changes; Scheduling's keyed capacity commands replay
by key while unkeyed release and busy reconciliation recover by snapshot
compare; Document Store follows its single-exchange-per-call rule.
Credentials and verified grant material stay host-owned and outside
