The patch contains state-loss, scheduling, pagination, and retry defects, and the CI classifier fails on the new workspace members. Focused Rust suites and the port-table check passed, but the dependency-direction tests failed; PostgreSQL suites were not run.

Full review comments:

- [P1] Propagate dry-run mode into the pass options — /Volumes/CodexWorktrees/adapter-components/crates/registry-adapter-job/src/main.rs:143-145
  Running `adapter-job pass --dry-run` leaves `options.dry_run` false. Although the preview destination receives the items, `run_pass` takes a lease, stages rows, records its synthetic acknowledgements, and advances the cursor. A subsequent real pass can therefore skip items that were never delivered. Pass the flag into the executor, including the loop path, rather than using it only to choose the destination and format output.

- [P1] Register both new crates in the CI shard inventory — /Volumes/CodexWorktrees/adapter-components/Cargo.toml:92-93
  Adding these workspace members without updating `.github/scripts/ci_changes.py::SHARDS` breaks the change classifier before it selects any jobs. Running it against the current Cargo metadata fails with `missing=['registry-adapter-connectors', 'registry-adapter-job']`. Register both crates in the appropriate shard and update the routing tests so the PR can reach its verification jobs.

- [P1] Keep the lease alive throughout destination delivery — /Volumes/CodexWorktrees/adapter-components/crates/registry-adapter-job/src/pass.rs:458-459
  The lease covers only the page-read deadline plus 60 seconds, but the pass subsequently delivers every item sequentially without renewal or an overall delivery deadline. For example, 100 successful four-second fixture deliveries outlive a 120-second lease. Another process can then acquire the job while the original pass still writes acknowledgements and unconditionally clears the lease or cursor. Renew and fence lease ownership throughout delivery, or enforce a bound covering the entire pass.

- [P1] Apply the file polling cap after the saved position — /Volumes/CodexWorktrees/adapter-components/crates/registry-adapter/src/engine/component/file.rs:260-262
  Truncating the complete sorted candidate list before applying the cursor permanently hides every file beyond `maxMessagesPerPoll`. Because reads leave consumed files in place, a directory with 501 files and the default cap of 500 repeatedly scans the same first 500 and never reaches the last file. Apply the per-poll limit to remaining candidates and retain a continuation when more files exist.

- [P1] Preserve rescan mode until the complete scan finishes — /Volumes/CodexWorktrees/adapter-components/crates/registry-adapter-job/src/main.rs:176-177
  Rescan mode lasts for only the first page: that page stamps `last_rescan_at`, and subsequent pages run with `rescan=false`, so unchanged refused items outside page one are never retried. If the rescan delay expires before traversal finishes, the loop also resets to page one again, potentially starving the source's tail indefinitely. Persist an in-progress rescan marker across pages and stamp completion only at the end of the scan.

- [P2] Anchor the initial delay to a fixed startup time — /Volumes/CodexWorktrees/adapter-components/crates/registry-adapter-job/src/trigger.rs:121-125
  For a fresh ordered-source job with a positive `initialDelayMilliseconds`, this always returns the full initial delay relative to the latest `now`. The loop sleeps, continues, and computes the same positive delay again because no pass has set `last_pass_at`. Consequently the first pass never runs. Track a fixed startup deadline or consume the initial delay once.

- [P2] Restore the saved prior reference during redelivery — /Volumes/CodexWorktrees/adapter-components/crates/registry-adapter-job/src/store.rs:486-491
  After an acknowledged digest changes, staging moves its `run_id` into `prior_run_id` and clears `run_id`. If that changed delivery crashes or stops before acknowledgement, this redelivery path reads the cleared `run_id` instead of the saved `prior_run_id`. The retry therefore changes `priorRunId` from the previous destination reference to null, violating identical-byte replay and losing the correlation needed to handle the edit. Read and preserve the stored prior reference in both staging and preview.

- [P2] Read the execution cursor after acquiring the lease — /Volumes/CodexWorktrees/adapter-components/crates/registry-adapter-job/src/pass.rs:417-418
  The cursor used by the pass is read before lease acquisition. A competing pass can acquire the lease, finish a page, and release it between this read and `take_lease`; the waiting process then acquires the free lease but executes the stale cursor and can overwrite the newer position. Acquire the lease and return its state atomically, or reread the execution state after acquisition.

- [P2] Persist the refusal before stopping at its limit — /Volumes/CodexWorktrees/adapter-components/crates/registry-adapter-job/src/pass.rs:270-276
  The item that reaches `refusal_limit` returns before `record_refused`, leaving its staged row `pending` without the destination's refusal code. An ordinary subsequent pass consequently redelivers that unchanged refused item even though only rescans are supposed to retry refused rows, and status reports the wrong outcome. Persist the refusal before evaluating the pass-stop condition.

- [P2] Validate the SQL statement root instead of counting SELECT nodes — /Volumes/CodexWorktrees/adapter-components/crates/registry-adapter/src/engine/component/postgres.rs:104-107
  The AST walk counts nested `SelectStmt` nodes rather than top-level statements. A focused probe confirms that valid read-only subqueries and SELECT CTEs are rejected, while `CREATE TEMP TABLE x AS SELECT 1` and `EXPLAIN SELECT 1` are accepted. Require exactly one top-level SELECT and inspect its descendants separately for forbidden operations, preserving both ordinary read queries and the compile-time refusal of utility statements.

- [P2] Update the dependency-gate test for the job command tree — /Volumes/CodexWorktrees/adapter-components/products/adapter/scripts/check_dependency_direction.py:125-128
  Adding `registry-adapter-job` to this allowlist leaves `test_cli_docs_carries_only_the_engine_command_trees` expecting the previous three-crate set. Running `python3 products/adapter/scripts/test_dependency_direction.py` now fails that assertion. Update the expected allowlist and graph fixture alongside the deliberate CLI-docs dependency so the boundary suite remains executable.
